ound  the visuals they're seeing on tv it is  adding to the mental health tool  i'd like to talk a bit about the  frontline workers the medical  professionals doctors  nurses medical attendants who are  literally at the front line of this  crisis and have been  for more than a year we were just  talking to a doctor from mumbai who was  trying to explain to us what they go  through on a daily basis  what is the impact on medical health  professionals such as these  well there is a lot of anxiety there is  some  palpable fear as well they have seen  many colleagues get infected  they know that they are taking great  personal risk in offering care obviously  but  you know given the kind of profession  they are in they obviously prioritize  patient care over  their personal well-being but it does  come at a cost because every day when  they go back home they are fearful about  taking  back the infection to their families  many of them live with young ones many  of them live with  elderly family members and it is quite  stressful you know we've seen many  health providers unfortunately dry  during this pandemic  and you know many of them continue to  get sick  and they don't really have a breathing  space because there is a shortage of  health professionals  so every day they have to go and do work  in these kind of stressful situations  where  it's a matter of life and death almost  every minute and to have to take  decisions around triage  around saying no to individuals when  there is not a bed available not enough  oxygen available  can be incredibly traumatic do people  have any recourse  to help or assistance  well you know it helps obviously to talk  about it  people are also you know reaching out to  other professionals a lot of mental  health professionals have stepped up  and are trying to be available you know  everyone's uh  trying their best to be able to be there  uh these are  incredibly uh tough times for everyone  but  uh you know the health provider  co